WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.416
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:10.248 --> 00:00:11.230
ALEXIS COX: Hey, Carl.

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:11.500
CARL KRAUSS: Hey.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:12.790
ALEXIS COX: Thanks for taking
me through your demo today.

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:13.850
CARL KRAUSS: Yeah, of course.

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:15.340
ALEXIS COX: Can you tell me a
little bit about what this is

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:16.160
and how it works?

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:16.910
CARL KRAUSS: Sure.

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:20.740
This is a 3D representation
of the architecture diagram

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:23.670
for the industrial asset
maintenance with Google Cloud

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.060
IoT and AR.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:28.340
This is an app that shows
an industrial device

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:31.850
that we have overlaid
augmented reality on top of it.

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:34.660
And so what this is, it allows
you to see sensors inside

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:35.470
of this.

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:38.350
And this device
connects into IoT Core.

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:41.890
So I'll show you a quick
demo of how this works.

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:45.550
And so you can see, in
here, we have sensors.

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:48.430
And I can trigger
a fault. And you

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:53.800
can see that that fault is
directed back into IoT Core.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:57.000
And you can see it live,
realtime on this display.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:57.827
ALEXIS COX: Cool.

00:00:57.827 --> 00:01:00.160
Can you tell me a little bit
about the technology that's

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:01.840
going on behind the scenes here?

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:02.590
CARL KRAUSS: Sure.

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:06.880
So inside of here, there is
some sensors that are securely

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:08.410
connecting to IoT Core.

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:10.570
And they're using
public/private keys,

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:15.460
using MKGT that are connecting,
and sending data into IoT Core.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:18.720
And IoT Core is a
secure device registry.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.580
It's a service that Google Cloud
offers for connecting things

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:25.120
like this asset that we showed
earlier, but could potentially

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.640
connect hundreds of
thousands of devices.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.880
And so now we have IoT Core.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:31.640
Now all of these things
can be configured.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:35.030
And data can be sent
through to Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:38.720
So Pub/Sub is a public
subscriber messaging queue

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:43.000
that allows data to be
buffered in a large bucket,

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.400
and for subscribers to
come through, and pull

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.900
that data off, and do some
sort of translation on it.

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:51.220
So as we walk down here,
we'll see that messages

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:52.510
are put onto a topic.

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:55.511
And they're duplicated
for different subscribers.

00:01:55.511 --> 00:01:57.010
There's kind of two
different things

00:01:57.010 --> 00:01:58.336
that you can do after Pub/Sub.

00:01:58.336 --> 00:01:59.710
So first we have
Cloud Functions.

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:02.140
And Cloud Functions is for
maybe more simple tasks,

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:05.365
like taking an event, unpacking
it, and then maybe writing it

00:02:05.365 --> 00:02:07.090
to a database.

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:09.789
Cloud Dataflow could be used
for maybe a little bit more

00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:11.770
complex, maybe ETL jobs.

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:14.080
And so in this
example, we're doing--

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.340
taking the last five
pieces of sensor data,

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:19.120
averaging them together, and
then writing that to Firebase.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:20.578
And the reason
we're using Firebase

00:02:20.578 --> 00:02:24.320
is for that realtime aspect
on those mobile devices.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:28.364
And so when that AR overlay
is laid on top of that device,

00:02:28.364 --> 00:02:29.530
the sensor data is realtime.

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:32.240
So if you see a fault that
we talked about earlier,

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:33.430
you can see that realtime.

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:36.910
And a technician may be able
to debug or troubleshoot that

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:38.100
using this technology.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:38.810
ALEXIS COX: That's really cool.

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:39.800
Thank you so much
for the walkthrough.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:41.050
CARL KRAUSS: Yeah, no problem.

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:43.750
[MUSIC PLAYING]

